group_by()


Flags the rows of a data frame as belonging to a group defined by a variable, for use in downstream operations.



star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height)`
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo               1.75
2 Tatooine            1.75

group_by() with summarize()

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height)`
  &lt;chr&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo               1.75
2 Tatooine            1.75




Makes a summary row for each group.

group_by() + summarize() + n()

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    summarize(n = n())



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld     n
  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Naboo         2
2 Tatooine      2




Same result as count(star_wars, homeworld) — count() is a shortcut for this exact pattern.

Statefulness
What will this produce?


star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    mutate(height_z = (height - mean(height)) / sd(height)) |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height_z))



# A tibble: 2 × 2
  homeworld `mean(height_z)`
  &lt;chr&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Naboo                    0
2 Tatooine                 0





Once grouped, a data frame stays grouped until reduced to one-row-per group or it is ungrouped.


can break the pipe after mutate to show it is still grouped

Statefulness
What will this produce?

star_wars |&gt;
    group_by(homeworld) |&gt;
    mutate(height_z = (height - mean(height)) / sd(height)) |&gt;
    ungroup() |&gt;
    summarize(mean(height_z))



# A tibble: 1 × 1
  `mean(height_z)`
             &lt;dbl&gt;
1                0

Building a function

c_to_f &lt;- function(temp_c) {
  (9/5) * temp_c + 32
}


Annotate with function name, arguments, expression.
Drop: - run ls(): appears in environment - add temp_c to first line to show it will not be returned

greet &lt;- function(name) {
  paste0("Hello ", name, "! Welcome to class.")
}

name_vec &lt;- c("larry", "moe", "curly")
name_vec &lt;- sample(name_vec)

for (name in name_vec) {
  print(greet(name))
  Sys.sleep(8)
}

course_grade &lt;- function(ps, proj_studio, quiz, final) {
  grade &lt;- 0.01 * mean(ps) + 0.20 * mean(proj_studio) + 
    0.49 * mean(quiz) + 0.3 * final
  return(grade)
}

course_grade &lt;- function(ps = 0, proj_studio = 0, quiz = 0, final = 0,
                         ps_w = .01, proj_studio_w = .2, quiz_w = .49, final_w = .3) {
  grade &lt;- ps_w * mean(ps) + proj_studio_w * mean(proj_studio) + 
    quiz_w * mean(quiz) + final_w * final
  return(grade)
}




How else can we generalize this function for other policies?

I’m looking for someone to mention this fact that the grading policy drops the lowest quiz. This will be a question on the problem set.

