if-else Statement


Executes one block of code if a condition is met and a different block if it is not — exactly one branch always runs.




x &lt;- sample(x = -10:10, size = 1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else {
  print("x is negative :(")
}


Similarly, run it multiple times and annotate code with different colors: if-then syntax, logical condition, and what to do.

Nested ifs


x &lt;- sample(x = -10:10, size = 1)

if (x &gt; 0) {
  print("x is positive :)")
} else if (x &lt; 0) {
  print("x is negative :(")
} else {
  print("x is zero :|")
}


Run it multiple times, then get the zero to toggle using x &lt;- 0

if (bike_working) {
  print("ride bike")
} else if (enough_time) {
  print("ride bus")
} else {
  print("call cab")
}


If time you can set the values of bike_working and enough_time to verify that it works.

For Loop


Repeats a block of code once for each element of a vector, with a loop variable taking each value in turn.



Prof. Sanchez Slides




for (item in vector) {
    perform_action
}

Option 1

for (i in 1:length(x)) {
  if (x[i] &gt; 0) {
    print("x is positive :)")
  }
}



[1] "x is positive :)"

Option 2

for (i in 1:length(x)) {
  if (x[i] &gt; 0) {
    print(paste("element", i, "is positive :)"))
  }
}



[1] "element 2 is positive :)"

Option 3

for (x_elem in x) {
  if (x_elem &gt; 0) {
    print(paste(x_elem, "is positive :)"))
  }
}



[1] "1.49400221271137 is positive :)"
